.TH DMA_ALLOC 2
.SH NAME
dma_alloc, dma_free \- memory for devices, for drivers (SYS_DMA_ALLOC, SYS_DMA_FREE)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include "mk.h"

void *dma_alloc(size_t size, uint64_t *pa);
long dma_free(void *va, size_t size);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B dma_alloc
gives the calling driver
.I size
bytes of physically contiguous, zeroed memory, which a device reads or
writes by itself (DMA), and stores its physical address in
.IR *pa ,
to give to the device. It counts in the process's memory quota and goes
with the process. The zeroes are written to memory first, for devices
that do not see the caches.
.PP
.I size
may be or-ed with
.BR DMA_LOW :
the memory must lie in the first GiB of RAM (the Raspberry Pis' firmware
and older devices see no more). At most 4096 pages (16 MiB) per call.
.PP
.B dma_free
gives back
.I size
bytes at
.IR va ,
which a
.B dma_alloc
returned. A dead driver's buffers are kept a while before reuse
(a device may still write to them).
.PP
On arm64, a buffer shared with a device that does not see the caches is
kept in step with
.B dma_sync
(mk.h). Drivers only.
.SH RETURN VALUE
.BR dma_alloc :
the address in the caller's memory, or a negative error (cast to a
pointer).
.BR dma_free :
0, or a negative error.
.SH ERRORS
.TP
.B EPERM
The caller is not a driver.
.TP
.B ENOMEM
.RB ( dma_alloc )
.I size
is 0 or above 16 MiB, the quota is reached, or no contiguous memory is
free.
.TP
.B EFAULT
.RB ( dma_alloc )
.I pa
is not writable.
.TP
.B EINVAL
.RB ( dma_free )
.I va
is not page-aligned or not in the driver's device area.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR map_phys (2),
.BR irq_bind (2),
.BR sys_spawn (2)
